  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Prolonged period of dry weather continues for the \nremainder of the week along with a large diurnal temperature range.\n\nUpper level ridging and surface high pressure continues to govern \nthe overall pattern through the remainder of the week. This will \nkeep conditions dry overall with a large diurnal temperature range. \n925 mb temperatures increase slightly into the week, reaching 10-12C \nand generally remaining there. This combined with continued overall \nW flow will keep the region warmer than normal with highs into the \nlow and even mid 60s. Mostly clear skies with light to calm winds, \nespecially tonight and Wednesday night, will be supportive of strong \nradiational cooling that will drop lows in the traditional cooler \nspots into 20s. Elsewhere, lows will generally fall to the 30s with \nthe coastal areas slightly warmer.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Potential for some rain sometime Sun into Mon if a \ncoastal low pressure system tracks close enough to the coast, but \nthat remains uncertain.\n\nA vigorous bit of southern stream energy emerges from the Gulf late \nin the week towards early next week. Given its origin, it would be \nloaded with moisture, though guidance is still quite inconclusive \nwhen it comes to how this would track; this system may not even \ntrack up along the coast. Some ensembles and their members do have \nit making its way up the coast and potentially impacting southern \nNew England towards the start of next week, however, there is still \nsignificant uncertainty given the amount of guidance that says \notherwise and tracks it out to sea. We will have to keep an eye on \nit over the coming days for a clearer picture to come into view.",
